  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">CORAL REEF CONSERVATION IN THE MEXICAN PACIFIC II</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The goal of this project is to conserve 686,767 acres of coral reefs in six Natural Protected Areas (NPAs) of the Mexican Pacific. The objective is to improve the management of coral reefs to reduce threats from uncontrolled touristic activities. The following activities will enhance and further develop coral reef conservation initiatives in the Mexican Pacific and include: (1) trainings and logistic support for park rangers in the Huatulco National Park (HNP) to install an effective conservation mooring buoy system; (2) trainings for CONANP staff working in six NPAs to implement coral reef monitoring, as well as data management and analysis, (3) a series of participatory workshops with local tourism operators and community watch groups to promote the proper use of the buoy system and tourism best practices in HNP, (4) organize an exchange of experiences between tourism providers in the HNP and the Cabo Pulmo National Park (CPNP) to foster and strengthen coral reef observation best practices, and (5) increase the awareness of 2,500 visitors to the HNP and the PNCP through informal lectures, workshops, graphic materials and outreach activities to promote coral reef conservation.</narrative>
  </description>
